<BaseLayout title="Las Vegas, Again — Huanyu Chen"
  description="Seven visits to Las Vegas between 2022 and 2026: a fake Venice, the STRAT twice, the Sphere before and after it lit up, a dawn walk down the Strip with our dog, and a lot of shows.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/misc">← Misc</a> · 2022–2026</p>
  <h1>Las Vegas, Again</h1>
  <p class="muted trip-sub">Seven trips in four years to the same four miles of boulevard, and what changed each time.</p>

  <div class="callout trip-stats">
    <strong>7 visits · March 2022 – March 2026</strong><br />
    March 2022 · June 2022 · April 2023 · September 2023 · June 2024 · August 2025 · March 2026<br />
    The STRAT's deck twice · the High Roller three times · the Sphere seen dark, unfinished, lit up and from the
    freeway, and once from the inside · Adele, KÀ, <em>O</em>, David Copperfield and <em>Mad Apple</em> · one dog
  </div>

  <figure class="trip-fig wide-fig">
    <a href={P + 'map-strip.jpg'}><img src={P + 'map-strip.jpg'} alt="Map of the Las Vegas Strip from the STRAT in the north to Mandalay Bay in the south, with photo thumbnails at the STRAT, Wynn, the Venetian, the Sphere, the High Roller, Caesars Palace, New York-New York, Luxor and Mandalay Bay, and the years each place appears in the photos." loading="lazy" /></a>
    <figcaption>The places that keep turning up in the photos, and the years they appear.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    I have been to Las Vegas seven times. People usually find this number a little surprising, and the
    honest answer to "why Vegas so often?" turns out to be short. The luxury hotels are surprisingly affordable, far more
    than the same standard would be almost anywhere else, and the shows are wonderful. That's really all it is: a city
    that keeps making a very nice weekend easy to say yes to.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interesting part is what a repeat visitor gets to see. One trip to Las Vegas shows you a city. Seven trips show
    you a city changing. Between March 2022 and March 2026 a black dome beside the Venetian sat dark on the skyline, and then
    switched on and became the most photographed object in town. A blue tower at the north end sat
    unfinished for well over a decade and then opened. And the same few landmarks (the STRAT, the
    Venetian's canals, the High Roller, the Bellagio) appear again and again, each time in a different light, in a
    different season, with a different person or dog beside me. This post walks through the visits in order. Three of
    them already have posts of their own, so those get a paragraph and a link.
  </p>

  <h2>1. March 2022: the bookends</h2>
  <p>
    The first visit in the record wasn't really a Vegas trip. Las Vegas was the airport and the bookends of a desert
    road trip to Death Valley, Horseshoe Bend and Antelope Canyon. In between the canyons there was a morning at the
    brand-new Resorts World, brunch at the Wynn, the STRAT's observation deck at night and a ride on the High Roller on
    the last afternoon. All of that is told in <a href="/misc/desert-2022">Death Valley, Vegas &amp; Page</a>. Two
    photos from that trip didn't make it into that post, and they belong here, because both places come back later.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-high-roller-cabin.jpg'} alt="A glass High Roller cabin on the wheel's white rim, high above the hotel towers of the Strip under a cloudy sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The next cabin up on the High Roller, March 16, 2022.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-skypod-sign.jpg'} alt="A wall poster advertising the STRAT's SkyPod, listing its levels: thrill rides, SkyJump, observation decks, a restaurant and a wedding chapel." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The STRAT's SkyPod, explained floor by floor.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The High Roller is a 550-foot observation wheel that opened in 2014 behind the LINQ. A full turn takes about half an
    hour, slow enough that the cabin ahead of you seems to hang still over the city. The STRAT's poster is a
    good summary of what Vegas is like. The top of an 1,149-foot tower holds thrill rides that dangle you over
    the edge, a place to jump off it on a wire, two observation decks, a revolving restaurant and, a few floors down, a
    wedding chapel. I took the elevator to the decks, which is as far as I have ever gone.
  </p>

  <h2>2. June 2022: a week on the Strip</h2>
  <p>
    Three months later we were back, and this time Las Vegas was the whole point. We drove up I-15 on Monday, June 13th,
    and stayed until Friday, five days in the middle of a June week. That is long enough to stop rushing, and long enough
    to learn the walk between the big resorts by heart. The first evening was spent wandering. At 7:42 pm the atrium on
    the Venetian side of the boulevard had a giant red "LOVE" planted in a garden under a glass dome. Later, a waterfall
    garden was lit up in the dark, white lamps shaped like mushrooms leaning over it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-love-atrium.jpg'} alt="A grand atrium with a glass dome, columns and a flower garden spelling LOVE in large red letters." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An atrium in full bloom, 7:42 pm.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-waterfall-night.jpg'} alt="A lit waterfall cascading over rocks at night, with white mushroom-shaped lamps in the foreground." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>A waterfall garden after dark.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    At 10:15 pm we reached the canal outside the <strong>Venetian</strong>. The resort opened in 1999 on the site of the
    old Sands, and its front is a pocket-sized Venice. The pink-and-white Doge's Palace, the Campanile and the Rialto
    Bridge are all reproduced at reduced scale and all lined up along a turquoise canal. At night, floodlit from below,
    the copy is better than it has any right to be. Gondolas were tied up for the night, and the water glowed
    green-blue from lamps set into the bottom of the canal.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-venetian-night.jpg'} alt="The Venetian's floodlit Doge's Palace façade at night, reflected in a turquoise canal with moored gondolas, the hotel tower behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Doge's Palace, Nevada edition, 10:15 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Fifteen minutes later we were standing in front of <strong>Sweet Sin</strong>, a dessert shop by the pastry chef
    Claude Escamilla, with polka-dot walls, bubble-letter signage and a glass counter of macarons, crêpes and cakes.
    Vegas is good at this sort of place: a shop designed to be photographed first and eaten in second.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-sweet-sin.jpg'} alt="The front of the Sweet Sin dessert shop at night, with colorful bubble letters, polka-dot walls and a pink-lit pastry counter inside." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Sweet Sin, 10:30 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next morning set the pattern for the rest of the week, which was mostly food. Brunch at the <strong>Wynn</strong>
    was smoked salmon with capers, cucumber and a bagel on the side, and then, at 1:03 pm, a slice of chocolate cake with
    more layers than I could count without zooming in. Nobody needs a cake that tall, which is exactly the point.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-salmon-brunch.jpg'} alt="A plate of smoked salmon with a halved egg, tomato, radish and cucumber, with a bagel and cream cheese in the background." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Brunch at the Wynn, June 14th.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-chocolate-cake.jpg'} alt="A towering slice of chocolate layer cake with chocolate chips pressed into the side." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The cake. Many layers.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    In the late afternoon we were back inside the Venetian, in the <strong>Grand Canal Shoppes</strong>. This is the
    famous part: a second Venice, upstairs and indoors, with an indoor canal, stone bridges, shopfronts dressed as palazzos and a ceiling painted as a
    blue sky with drifting clouds. The light never changes. It is always about five in the afternoon on a fine day in
    there, which is disorienting in a pleasant way, and it was in fact five in the afternoon when I took this.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-grand-canal.jpg'} alt="The indoor Grand Canal Shoppes: a canal with a gondola, an iron railing, Venetian-style shopfronts and a painted blue sky ceiling." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Indoors, under a painted sky, 4:59 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next daylight photo is the outdoor version of the same idea. At 6:03 pm on Wednesday the real sky was a hard
    desert blue, and the Rialto Bridge copy and the red-brick Campanile stood up against it on the Strip side of the
    resort. The original Rialto is a stone bridge over the Grand Canal in Venice, finished in 1591. This one crosses a
    moving walkway and some palm trees. It is still a nice bridge, and the bell tower behind it is close
    enough to the real one that it looks right from most angles.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-rialto-bridge.jpg'} alt="The Venetian's replica Rialto Bridge and red-brick bell tower against a deep blue sky, with an ornate street lamp." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Rialto Bridge and the Campanile, June 15th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    One night we drove north up Las Vegas Boulevard after midnight, toward the old end of town. At 12:40 am the
    <strong>STRAT</strong> was lit gold from top to bottom, standing over a row of souvenir shops, liquor stores and a
    small Ferris-wheel sign. This stretch between the big resorts and downtown is the least glamorous part of the
    Strip, and at that hour it was nearly empty. It was the same tower I had stood on top of three months earlier, now
    seen from the street.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-strat-midnight.jpg'} alt="The STRAT tower lit gold at night above a row of souvenir and liquor stores on Las Vegas Boulevard." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The STRAT at 12:40 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-strat-road.jpg'} alt="An empty boulevard at night leading toward the gold-lit STRAT tower, with neon signs on both sides." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Driving toward it.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The last big dinner of the week was on Thursday, near the Venetian, and it began with a tower of thinly sliced
    zucchini stacked like a pile of coins, before grilled octopus and lamb chops. That week had plenty of the usual Vegas, too: frozen
    drinks in absurdly tall plastic cups, brunch at the Bellagio and an afternoon at the fountains. What it did not have,
    for me, was a casino floor. I turned 21 just after that trip, so for five days in the most famous gambling town on
    earth I was politely and repeatedly turned away: by casino security at the edge of the carpet, by nightclub
    doormen, by anyone who checked an ID. The lesson, if there is one, is to plan your big Vegas week for a few weeks
    later. The food, at least, is what the camera roll remembers best.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2022-zucchini-stack.jpg'} alt="A tall stack of thin fried zucchini slices on a white plate, topped with herbs." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Zucchini, stacked, June 16th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On Friday we drove home. At 12:57 pm, south of Jean on I-15, I took a photo out of the car window of a flat, dusty
    plain with a row of tiny bright dots on the far side. I didn't know what they were then. Looking at it now, after
    the next visit, I'm fairly sure they are <strong>Seven Magic Mountains</strong>, seen from the freeway as a smudge
    of color. It took another ten months before we actually pulled off and walked up to them.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2022-seven-magic-distance.jpg'} alt="A hazy desert flat seen from a moving car on I-15, with a row of small colorful shapes on the horizon below mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Bright dots on the horizon, June 17th, 12:57 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>3. April 2023: from the top</h2>
  <p>
    The third visit was a short weekend, Saturday and Sunday, April 8th and 9th, which was Easter weekend, and once
    again we drove. It started at 10:36 am with a view from a high window over the south-central Strip. The curved, blue-glass
    <strong>Vdara</strong> tower filled one side of the frame, with its name in white script near the top. Behind it, the
    city ran out flat to the mountains. CityCenter, the cluster of glass towers that Vdara belongs to, opened in 2009 and
    is the most modern-looking part of the Strip. It looks less like a casino resort and more like a business district that
    happens to have slot machines.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2023-vdara.jpg'} alt="The curved blue-glass Vdara hotel tower with its name in script, beside a view over the flat city to distant mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Vdara, 10:36 am, April 8th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That afternoon we went back up the <strong>STRAT</strong>, a year after my first visit, this time by daylight.
    At 2:54 pm the view north and east was all detail that the night hides: parking lots, construction sites, rows of
    low-rise motels and the blue and red towers of the north Strip. The deck is enclosed by tilted glass, and the
    window frames show up in half the photos. It is also the best place in the city to see how much of Las Vegas is
    simply flat, empty lots waiting for the next big idea.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2023-strat-deck.jpg'} alt="Daytime aerial view from the STRAT over the north end of the Strip: towers in blue glass and red, empty lots and parking, mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>North Strip from the STRAT's deck, 2:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Two of those big ideas are in the photos, and neither was finished. On the left, past a golf course, sits a huge
    black dome. That is the <strong>Sphere</strong>, still dark in April 2023, five months before it opened in
    September with U2. In the middle stands a tall blue-glass tower, the <strong>Fontainebleau</strong>. Its
    construction began in the mid-2000s and stalled after the 2008 crash, and the shell then stood empty on the
    skyline for more than a decade. It finally opened in December 2023. When I took this photo, both were still
    waiting for opening day.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2023-sphere-unopened.jpg'} alt="Close-up from the STRAT: a giant dark dome beyond a golf course, beside a tall unfinished blue-glass tower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sphere, unlit, and the Fontainebleau, unopened, April 2023.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2023-strat-frame.jpg'} alt="The view from the STRAT's observation deck framed by the dark edge of a tilted window." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The deck's tilted glass, in the corner of the frame.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    On Easter Sunday afternoon, driving home, we finally pulled off I-15 at the dots. <strong>Seven Magic
    Mountains</strong> is a public artwork by the Swiss artist Ugo Rondinone, installed in 2016 in the dry lakebed
    south of the city. It is seven totems of stacked limestone boulders, each about thirty feet tall and painted in
    fluorescent pinks, yellows, greens and blues. Up close they look like candy, and against the brown desert and the
    grey mountains they are almost too bright to be real. At 4:24 pm there was a steady procession of visitors
    walking up the path from the parking lot.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2023-seven-magic.jpg'} alt="Seven tall stacks of fluorescent painted boulders in the desert, with visitors walking along a path and mountains behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Seven Magic Mountains, up close at last, April 9th.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>4. September 2023: Adele</h2>
  <p>
    The fourth visit was the one built around a ticket. On Labor Day weekend 2023 we drove up I-15 through a storm and a
    double rainbow to see Adele's residency at the Colosseum at Caesars Palace, saw <em>KÀ</em> at the MGM Grand, rode
    the High Roller at night, and on the way home turned off the freeway at Zzyzx. The Sphere was a few weeks from opening. That trip has its own post: <a href="/misc/labor-day-2023">Rainbows, Adele and
    Zzyzx</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. June 2024: showing my parents</h2>
  <p>
    The fifth visit was for my parents. They had come from China to visit me, and before they flew home I wanted to
    take them somewhere amazing, somewhere that would feel like nowhere else. After three trips of my own, I knew
    exactly where that was. It was a short visit, Sunday and Monday, June 9th and 10th, 2024, and we packed it with
    the thing Las Vegas does best: shows.
  </p>
  <p>
    We saw <em>O</em>, Cirque du Soleil's water show at the <strong>Bellagio</strong>, where acrobats dive into and
    rise out of a pool that is also the stage, and the floor seems to turn from water to solid ground and back. We saw
    <strong>David Copperfield</strong> at the MGM Grand, which was really for my dad, who is a big fan. And we went inside the <strong>Sphere</strong> for <em>Postcard from
    Earth</em>, Darren Aronofsky's film made for the venue, which premiered there in October 2023. It was the first
    time inside the Sphere for all three of us, and the screen wraps up and over your head, which is a hard thing to
    describe to someone back home and an easy thing to just show them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Between the shows there was time to look around. The first photo, at 4:18 pm, looks down
    from a high window onto the long cream-colored tower of the Venetian, its rooftop pools and the flat city
    stretching away to the mountains. It is almost exactly the view I would have had from the boulevard two years
    earlier, turned on its side. When you come to a place often enough, even the high windows start to look familiar.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-afternoon-window.jpg'} alt="An afternoon view down onto the Venetian's long hotel tower and rooftop pools, with the city and mountains beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>An afternoon view over the Venetian, June 9th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Just before midnight the Sphere put on its other show, the one outside. The dome that had been dark on the skyline in
    2023 was now fully lit. Its exterior, the Exosphere, is covered in roughly 1.2 million LED pucks, and the whole
    366-foot ball works as a screen. It was cycling through animations, first a slowly turning faceted jewel and then,
    at 11:54 pm, a scene in deep blues and violets. From above, with the dark city all around, it looked
    like a planet that had landed in a parking lot.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-sphere-night.jpg'} alt="The Sphere at night seen from high above, its entire LED surface glowing with a blue and violet image, surrounded by city lights." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sphere, lit, 11:54 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The next afternoon we rode the <strong>High Roller</strong> again, my third time on the wheel in a little over two
    years. At 1:17 pm the view was the Flamingo's pool garden straight below, the long white Flamingo towers, the
    big resorts of the central Strip behind them, and the mountains on the horizon. It's the same view as March 2022, just with
    harder summer light. The view the other way, down onto the LINQ's rooftops, had something new in it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-high-roller-flamingo.jpg'} alt="View from the High Roller over the Flamingo's pool garden and white hotel towers, with more resort towers beyond." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Over the Flamingo from the High Roller, 1:17 pm, June 10th.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-high-roller-linq.jpg'} alt="View from the High Roller over flat rooftops and hotel towers toward distant mountains." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The other way, over the LINQ.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    That something was the <strong>Sphere</strong>, by daylight. With nothing on its screen, the dome is a dull
    dark grey, the LED grid only visible up close, and it sits in the landscape like a giant golf ball half sunk into
    the ground. Seen like this, it's remarkable how much it depends on the dark. At night it is the brightest thing in
    town. At 1:18 pm on a June afternoon it looked a little shy, a stage set waiting for its cue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-sphere-day.jpg'} alt="The Sphere by day seen from the High Roller: a large dark dome beyond flat rooftops, with mountains on the horizon." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Sphere at 1:18 pm, screen off.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Near the top of the turn the next cabin swung into the frame. The cabins are glass bubbles that hold up to forty
    people, big enough that they host parties, and from inside one the neighbouring cabin looks oddly like a space capsule
    parked over the Strip. Behind it at 1:19 pm were the hotel towers of the
    central Strip, and beyond those the desert.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-high-roller-cabin.jpg'} alt="A white High Roller cabin in the foreground, with the towers of the central Strip and a desert horizon behind." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The neighbouring cabin, 1:19 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The last photo of the visit is from 6:28 pm, looking down from high above onto <strong>Caesars Palace</strong>'s
    pool complex, the Garden of the Gods. A domed white temple stands in the middle of a round turquoise pool, with rows of orange loungers
    around it, and marble horses rear in the foreground. Caesars opened in 1966 and is one of the oldest resorts still
    standing on the Strip. Nine months earlier, at its Colosseum, we had seen Adele.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2024-caesars-pool.jpg'} alt="Caesars Palace's pool area from above: a domed temple in a round pool, orange loungers, palm trees, marble horse statues and the white hotel tower." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Garden of the Gods pools, 6:28 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>6. Labor Day 2025: with Wangcai</h2>
  <p>
    Two years after Adele, on Labor Day weekend again, we drove up with a new passenger: our dog,
    <a href="/misc/wangcai-2025">Wangcai</a>, a cream-colored dog who had joined the family earlier that year. We came in after
    dark on Friday, August 29th. At 8:09 pm the freeway gave the classic arrival view: the whole Strip lit up across the
    low ground to the east, gold towers, the High Roller ringed in light, and, at the edge of the frame, the Sphere
    glowing blue.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-strip-from-i15.jpg'} alt="The Strip at night seen from the freeway: gold hotel towers, the lit High Roller wheel and other casino lights above a concrete barrier." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Arriving on I-15, 8:09 pm, August 29th.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Dinner was in <strong>Chinatown</strong>, the long strip of Asian restaurants and malls on Spring Mountain Road just
    west of the Strip, and one of the most reliable reasons to visit Las Vegas that has nothing to do with casinos. The
    order was a spicy Sichuan dish of blood tofu, dark cubes in red chili oil under a heap of cilantro and scallions,
    which is not something you find on many menus and which was excellent.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2025-chinatown-tofu.jpg'} alt="A plate of dark blood tofu cubes in red chili oil with chopped cilantro and scallions." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Blood tofu in Chinatown, 8:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The best part of this visit was the next morning. A dog needs a walk whatever city it is in, so at 7:39 am, before
    the heat, Wangcai and I set out along the quiet south end of the Strip. In late August the desert is already warm
    at that hour, but the light is low and gold, and the boulevard is nearly empty. In the first photo Wangcai stopped to
    look at the gold towers of <strong>Mandalay Bay</strong> rising above the freeway. Twenty minutes later we reached
    its sign.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-wangcai-mandalay.jpg'} alt="A cream-colored dog on a pink leash standing on a curb, looking toward the gold towers of Mandalay Bay in early morning light." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Wangcai, considering Mandalay Bay, 7:39 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-wangcai-sign.jpg'} alt="A cream-colored dog seen from behind on a long sidewalk with long shadows, beside the Mandalay Bay sign and palm trees." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>At the Mandalay Bay sign, 8:00 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    <strong>Mandalay Bay</strong> opened in 1999 as the southern anchor of the Strip, and its three-winged gold tower
    catches the morning sun better than anything else on the boulevard. At 8:10 the façade was glowing above a
    landscaped rock garden of palms and boulders.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-mandalay-bay.jpg'} alt="Mandalay Bay's curved gold tower in morning sun above palm trees and a rock garden, under a clear blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mandalay Bay at 8:10 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    A few minutes up the sidewalk, the next resort came into view across an intersection. It was a black glass
    pyramid, the <strong>Luxor</strong>, with the overhead pedestrian bridges of the Strip strung across the road in
    front of it.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-luxor-crossing.jpg'} alt="The black Luxor pyramid across an empty intersection, with traffic signals, bollards and a pedestrian bridge." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Luxor across the intersection, 8:11 am.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-luxor-sphinx.jpg'} alt="The Luxor pyramid and its giant sphinx behind an avenue of small sphinx statues and a railing, under a deep blue sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The sphinx out front, 8:14 am.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    The Luxor opened in 1993, a thirty-story pyramid of dark glass with a beam of light on top that is said to be the
    strongest in the world. In front of it lies a sphinx, larger than the original at Giza, with a short avenue of
    smaller sphinxes leading up to the entrance. At eight in the morning, with nobody else around, it looked less like a casino and more like a film set before the
    crew arrives. Wangcai was much more interested in the patches of grass.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next door is the <strong>Excalibur</strong>, which opened in 1990 and still looks exactly like what it is, a hotel
    tower with a fairy-tale castle stuck to the front, all red and blue pointed turrets. Behind it, across Tropicana
    Avenue, the skyline of <strong>New York-New York</strong> appeared, and that's the moment the Strip
    stops pretending to be one place. In a quarter of an hour, on foot, we went from ancient Egypt to medieval England to
    Manhattan.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-excalibur.jpg'} alt="The white Excalibur hotel tower with red and blue castle turrets, and the New York-New York skyline beyond, across a wide boulevard." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Excalibur, with Manhattan behind it, 8:20 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    New York-New York opened in 1997 as a collage of a dozen Manhattan towers at roughly a third of their real size,
    the Empire State and Chrysler buildings among them, with a Statue of Liberty replica in front and a red roller
    coaster looping in and out of the whole thing. In the morning sun at 8:24, with the coaster track curling past
    the towers, it looked like a city skyline packed into a souvenir snow globe.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-nyny-liberty.jpg'} alt="New York-New York's miniature Manhattan skyline with a Statue of Liberty replica and the red track of a roller coaster, under a clear sky." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>New York-New York, 8:24 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    The walk ended at <strong>Hershey's Chocolate World</strong> on the corner of New York-New York, with two costumed
    candy statues out front, a Hershey bar and a pink Kiss, waving beside a miniature "Welcome to Las Vegas" sign.
    By 8:28 the sun was well up and the pavement was starting to warm, so we turned back. It was a little under an
    hour for four of the Strip's oldest themes. It is also, I think, the best way to see this part of the boulevard: early,
    empty and on foot, with a dog setting the pace.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2025-hersheys.jpg'} alt="The rounded storefront of Hershey's Chocolate World, with a costumed Hershey bar statue and a pink Kiss statue in front." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Hershey's Chocolate World, 8:28 am.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    That evening, at 6:49 pm, we were back at New York-New York for <em>Mad Apple</em>, Cirque du Soleil's show
    there, a love letter to New York City told with acrobatics, comedy and music. The photo shows the theatre before it
    started, bathed in purple and magenta light, a raised stage under a mirrored ball and rows of seats in the dark. It's
    a good reminder of the second half of the answer to "why Vegas?". On any given night
    the city has more good shows playing than most cities get in a season.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-nyny-stage.jpg'} alt="A theatre in the round lit in purple and magenta, with a raised stage, a mirror ball and an audience in the dark." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Before <em>Mad Apple</em> at New York-New York, 6:49 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On Sunday afternoon we went north to the <strong>Wynn</strong>. Its atrium is a garden under glass. That weekend it
    was hung with spheres made of flowers dangling from real trees, light slanting in through the roof, and at the center
    sat a large sculpture of a seated animal made of faceted, multicolored polygons hugging a polka-dot ball. I had last
    been inside the Wynn for brunch in 2022. The flowers had changed completely since then, and the Wynn changes them on
    purpose, season by season.
  </p>
  <div class="trip-pair">
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-wynn-atrium.jpg'} alt="The Wynn's atrium: trees under a glass roof hung with spheres made of red, pink and white flowers, with visitors walking below." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Wynn's atrium, 3:33 pm, August 31st.</figcaption></figure>
    <figure class="trip-fig"><img src={P + '2025-wynn-bear.jpg'} alt="A large sculpture of a seated animal made of colorful faceted polygons, holding a polka-dot ball, among flowers in the Wynn's atrium." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The atrium's centerpiece.</figcaption></figure>
  </div>
  <p>
    Half an hour later we were back in the <strong>Venetian</strong>, three years after the June 2022 week. This time
    the photo is from a balcony looking down into a multi-level shopping concourse with escalators, a reflecting pool,
    beds of red flowers and the shops of the Grand Canal Shoppes. Some places in Vegas get rebuilt every few years.
    This one looked exactly as I remembered it, painted sky and all.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2025-venetian-concourse.jpg'} alt="A grand multi-level indoor concourse seen from above, with escalators, a reflecting pool, red flowers and shoppers." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The Venetian, from a balcony, 4:00 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    Late that night, at 11:35 pm, I took a picture of a small souvenir: a <strong>Bruno Mars Live</strong> button
    pack, four round pins with photos of him singing and playing guitar, one more small object picked up along
    the way.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2025-bruno-buttons.jpg'} alt="A souvenir card reading Bruno Mars Live button pack, with four round pin badges showing the singer." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>The souvenir, 11:35 pm.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>
    On Labor Day itself, before the drive home, we stopped at a churro kiosk off the Strip. It was decorated with
    giant painted churros and a little "Welcome to Las Vegas" sign, and the box held loops of churros dusted in
    cinnamon sugar with three dipping sauces. It was not the most glamorous meal of the seven visits. It may be the one I
    would most like to repeat.
  </p>
  <figure class="trip-fig narrow-fig"><img src={P + '2025-churros.jpg'} alt="A box of looped churros dusted with cinnamon sugar beside three cups of dipping sauce." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Churros on Labor Day, 12:12 pm.</figcaption></figure>

  <h2>7. March 2026: a stop on the way home</h2>
  <p>
    The most recent visit was barely a visit at all. On the last day of a spring-break loop through the Southwest,
    Wangcai still along for the ride, we came down I-15 from St. George and stopped on Sunday, March 22nd, at the
    Bellagio Conservatory and the fountains. That story is in <a href="/misc/spring-break-2026">Hoodoos, Earthships and
    Red Rocks</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Same city, seven times</h2>
  <p>
    Lined up in order, the photos tell a story none of the individual trips did. The first time, Las Vegas was a place
    to land on the way to somewhere else. The second time, it was the destination, and the camera mostly pointed at
    plates. By the third, I was looking at the skyline from above and noticing what was missing. By the fifth, the black
    dome I had photographed unfinished was the brightest thing in town, and I was sitting inside it with my parents. And by the sixth, I was walking a dog past a
    pyramid at eight in the morning, which is not something anybody plans to do the first time they come here.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is what repetition buys you. The first visit to a place like Las Vegas is all spectacle, and it's easy to
    stop there and decide you've seen it. But the Strip is not finished. It gets rebuilt all the time, with new
    domes, new towers, new flowers in the Wynn's atrium and new names on the marquees, while a few old landmarks stay
    put and give you something to measure the change against. The STRAT and the Venetian's bridge haven't moved. Everything around them has.
  </p>
  <p>
    And the original answer still holds. The hotels are a treat, the shows are wonderful, and the drive up I-15 is
    just long enough to feel like going somewhere. I expect there will be an eighth time. I'm curious what will be
    new.
  </p>
